1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:03,000
Let's move now on to the

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,840
file system structure. So how is a file system
organizing different layers.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:18,289
The idea when you design a file system is
to have different layers for different functionalities.

4
00:00:18,289 --> 00:00:22,230
This is true for a file system, but I even
mentioned this in the very beginning of the

5
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:26,690
course for the entire operating system that
you can have different layers. It's good for

6
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:28,430
modularity and keeping

7
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:38,420
let's say different types of jobs in different
levels / in different models, so these are

8
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,929
basically the different layers you can have

9
00:00:41,929 --> 00:00:47,239
if you want to organize your file system in
let's say different parts, so you need the

10
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:51,270
lowest one so the one that is very close to
the device to be about I/O control. You

11
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:58,929
will need a part of the file system that is
the one that actually

12
00:00:58,929 --> 00:01:05,199
speaks this kind of language, which is read
a specific drive, maybe a specific cylinder

13
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:09,860
and track. This is of course for a rotating
disk, it doesn't apply to an SSD, but it's the

14
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:16,460
very low level IO layer that speaks basically
the language of the device.

15
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:24,180
On top of that, you have the basic file system
so called basic file system. It speaks in

16
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:31,060
terms of blocks, so it says retrieve a specific
block or write to a specific block and it's

17
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:36,110
also the one that maintains buffers and caches,
when you read and write to the disk, these

18
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:41,100
are similar in spirit to what we discussed
also for

19
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:44,280
the memory.

20
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:51,000
You have the file organization module and
this is about translating logical blocks into

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:03,300
physical blocks. So if you want to read the
fifth block of a file again to simplify the

22
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:08,030
life of the programmer the processes and
so on, the operating system separates between

23
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:11,950
logical and physical addresses, so

24
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:19,020
from the logical perspective, the fifth block
is the fifth block. It starts at 0, so it

25
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:23,790
will be blocked #4, but in practice this *file*
might be written in a disk in several

26
00:02:23,790 --> 00:02:28,790
position. The disk is not going to use
necessarily contiguous allocation, and I will

27
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:32,680
present this in the next video.

28
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:38,060
In this layer you also have the managing of
free space, so not only the translation between

29
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:43,480
logical and physical, but also this is the layer
that maintains information about where you

30
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:47,090
can actually write if you need to write something
new.

31
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:53,209
And here, of course, you start having understanding
of files too, not only blocks because

32
00:02:53,209 --> 00:02:55,250
of course you need to

33
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:00,939
have the concept of a file in order to understand
what is the logical block inside that file

34
00:03:00,939 --> 00:03:04,409
and translate that into the physical one.

35
00:03:04,409 --> 00:03:12,169
The highest one, the highest level you have just before
actually the applications that you run,

36
00:03:12,169 --> 00:03:17,909
is the logical file system. This is where
we keep all the metadata I introduced before

37
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:26,849
And now this is what we are going to discuss
next when we see at different ways of actually

38
00:03:26,849 --> 00:03:28,370
maintaining

39
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:33,079
stuff (data/files) into the disk.

40
00:03:33,079 --> 00:03:39,200
Before that I also want to mention that since
you have this separation of functionalities

41
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:45,700
in different layers, this is what allows you
to have virtual file systems. So in practice

42
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:48,919
you can have

43
00:03:48,919 --> 00:03:55,809
different file systems that are maintained
at the same time and they can be implemented

44
00:03:55,809 --> 00:04:00,959
in different ways. Maybe speaking to different
type of devices and so on. Since you have

45
00:04:00,959 --> 00:04:08,790
the different layers you can define also a layer
that is the virtual file system which does

46
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:09,799
once again

47
00:04:09,799 --> 00:04:16,430
acts as a middleware. Stays in between the
lower level interfaces and the higher ones,

48
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:19,840
the user, the processes,

49
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:26,820
to translate commands like open a file read a
file and so on from the general interface,

50
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:31,750
the common shared interface with the user
to the specific ones you have for each file

51
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:34,340
system.

52
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:41,630
The next thing we're going to look at in the
next video, it's how we actually implement

53
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:50,200
files. How do we keep them? If we have
a disk block n on our device,

54
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:52,500
how do we know

55
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,420
what is the file that is contained on that
block? We will discuss three different

56
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:05,950
ways, contiguous allocation, linked list
allocation also with the file allocation table

57
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:12,340
variant and I-nodes that are the ones you
actually use, for example, in your Unix

58
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:12,840
systems.

